# Global / Non-Member Operator Overloading in C++

> [!abstract] Topic
> **Operator Overloading as Non-Member (Global) Functions**
>
> In this approach, overloaded operators are defined **outside the class** rather than as member functions.
>
> Example:
>
> ```cpp
> bool operator==(const Mystring &lhs, const Mystring &rhs);
> Mystring operator-(const Mystring &obj);
> Mystring operator+(const Mystring &lhs, const Mystring &rhs);
> ```

---

## 1. Member vs Global Operator Overloading

### Member function

When an operator is a member function:

```cpp
class Mystring {
public:
    Mystring operator+(const Mystring &rhs) const;
};
```

It is called through the left-hand object:

```cpp
first + second;
```

Conceptually:

```cpp
first.operator+(second);
```

The left-hand operand is automatically `*this`.

---

### Global / Non-Member function

When the operator is a global function:

```cpp
Mystring operator+(const Mystring &lhs,
                   const Mystring &rhs);
```

Both operands must be explicitly provided:

```cpp
first + second;
```

Conceptually:

```cpp
operator+(first, second);
```

There is no `this` pointer because the function does not belong to the class.

---

# 2. Why Use Global Operators?

One important advantage is that the operator can work naturally when the **left-hand operand is not a `Mystring` object**.

For example:

```cpp
Mystring moe{"Moe"};

Mystring result = "Larry" + moe;
```

This is difficult with a member function because the left-hand side is:

```cpp
"Larry"
```

which is a C-style string, not a `Mystring` object.

A global operator can receive both operands:

```cpp
operator+("Larry", moe);
```

and the `Mystring` constructor can convert `"Larry"` into a temporary `Mystring`.

Therefore:

```cpp
"Larry" + moe;
```

can work.

---

# 3. `friend` Functions

The `str` member is private:

```cpp
class Mystring {
private:
    char *str;
};
```

A global function normally **cannot access private members**.

For example:

```cpp
bool operator==(const Mystring &lhs,
                const Mystring &rhs)
{
    return std::strcmp(lhs.str, rhs.str) == 0;
}
```

would normally be illegal because:

```cpp
lhs.str
rhs.str
```

are private.

We solve this by declaring the global function as a `friend`:

```cpp
class Mystring {

    friend bool operator==(const Mystring &lhs,
                            const Mystring &rhs);

private:
    char *str;
};
```

Now the global function is allowed to access private members.

> [!important]
> `friend` does **not** make the function a member function.
>
> It remains a global/non-member function.
>
> `friend` simply gives it permission to access the class's private and protected members.

---

# 4. Equality Operator

Global declaration:

```cpp
friend bool operator==(const Mystring &lhs,
                       const Mystring &rhs);
```

Definition:

```cpp
bool operator==(const Mystring &lhs,
                const Mystring &rhs)
{
    return std::strcmp(lhs.str, rhs.str) == 0;
}
```

Usage:

```cpp
Mystring larry{"Larry"};
Mystring moe{"Moe"};

if (larry == moe)
    ...
```

Conceptually:

```cpp
operator==(larry, moe);
```

Both operands are explicitly passed to the global function.

---

# 5. Unary Operator as Global Function

The unary minus operator can also be global:

```cpp
friend Mystring operator-(const Mystring &obj);
```

Definition:

```cpp
Mystring operator-(const Mystring &obj)
{
    // ...
}
```

Usage:

```cpp
Mystring larry{"Larry"};

larry = -larry;
```

Conceptually:

```cpp
operator-(larry);
```

There is only one operand because `-` is unary in this context.

---

# 6. Global Concatenation Operator

The `+` operator receives two operands:

```cpp
friend Mystring operator+(const Mystring &lhs,
                          const Mystring &rhs);
```

Definition:

```cpp
Mystring operator+(const Mystring &lhs,
                   const Mystring &rhs)
{
    // create concatenated string
    // return new Mystring
}
```

Usage:

```cpp
Mystring moe{"Moe"};
Mystring larry{"Larry"};

Mystring result = moe + larry;
```

Conceptually:

```cpp
operator+(moe, larry);
```

---

# 7. Why Global `+` Is Especially Useful

The important example:

```cpp
Mystring stooges = "Larry" + moe;
```

The left-hand side is a C-style string:

```cpp
"Larry"
```

The right-hand side is:

```cpp
moe
```

A global operator can accept both operands and allow the `Mystring` constructor to perform the conversion.

This makes expressions such as:

```cpp
Mystring two_stooges = moe + " " + "Larry";
```

possible.

And even:

```cpp
Mystring three_stooges =
    moe + " " + larry + " " + "Curly";
```

can work through repeated operator calls and temporary `Mystring` objects.

---

# 8. Operator Chaining

Consider:

```cpp
Mystring two_stooges = moe + " " + "Larry";
```

The operations happen sequentially.

Conceptually:

```cpp
(moe + " ") + "Larry"
```

First:

```cpp
moe + " "
```

produces a temporary `Mystring`.

Then:

```cpp
temporary + "Larry"
```

produces the final result.

This is one reason returning a `Mystring` object from `operator+` is important.

---

# 9. Basic Structure

A global operator typically has three parts.

### 1. Friend declaration inside the class

```cpp
class Mystring {

friend Mystring operator+(const Mystring &lhs,
                           const Mystring &rhs);

private:
    char *str;
};
```

### 2. Global definition outside the class

```cpp
Mystring operator+(const Mystring &lhs,
                   const Mystring &rhs)
{
    // implementation
}
```

### 3. Normal operator syntax

```cpp
Mystring result = first + second;
```

The user does not need to call:

```cpp
operator+(first, second);
```

although that is essentially what happens conceptually.

---

# 10. `lhs` and `rhs`

These names are conventions.

```cpp
lhs
```

means:

> **left-hand side**

```cpp
rhs
```

means:

> **right-hand side**

For:

```cpp
first + second
```

we have:

```text
lhs = first
rhs = second
```

For:

```cpp
larry == moe
```

we have:

```text
lhs = larry
rhs = moe
```

