# 11. Global Operators in This Project

The `Mystring` class currently demonstrates:

```cpp
friend bool operator==(const Mystring &lhs,
                       const Mystring &rhs);

friend Mystring operator-(const Mystring &obj);

friend Mystring operator+(const Mystring &lhs,
                          const Mystring &rhs);
```

These correspond to:

| Operator  | Purpose                         | Operands |
| --------- | ------------------------------- | -------: |
| `==`      | Compare string contents         |        2 |
| unary `-` | Convert characters to lowercase |        1 |
| `+`       | Concatenate strings             |        2 |

---

# 12. Important Difference

### Member operator

```cpp
class Mystring {
public:
    Mystring operator+(const Mystring &rhs) const;
};
```

Conceptually:

```cpp
first.operator+(second);
```

The left operand is the object:

```cpp
first
```

---

### Global operator

```cpp
Mystring operator+(const Mystring &lhs,
                   const Mystring &rhs);
```

Conceptually:

```cpp
operator+(first, second);
```

Both operands are parameters.

---

# 13. Why `friend` Is Useful Here

Without `friend`:

```cpp
bool operator==(const Mystring &lhs,
                const Mystring &rhs)
{
    return std::strcmp(lhs.str, rhs.str) == 0;
}
```

the function cannot access:

```cpp
lhs.str
rhs.str
```

because `str` is private.

With:

```cpp
friend bool operator==(const Mystring &lhs,
                       const Mystring &rhs);
```

it can access them.

Therefore:

```text
private data
      ↓
   friend
      ↓
global operator
```

---

# 14. Important Mental Model

The most useful way to think about this topic:

```cpp
a + b
```

does **not** literally mean that `+` is special magic.

C++ searches for an appropriate overloaded operator.

With a global operator:

```cpp
a + b
```

can conceptually become:

```cpp
operator+(a, b);
```

With a member operator:

```cpp
a + b
```

can conceptually become:

```cpp
a.operator+(b);
```

This distinction becomes particularly important when the left-hand operand is a different type.

---

# 15. Current `Mystring` Design

The project now demonstrates both styles:

### Member operators

```cpp
Mystring operator-() const;

Mystring operator+(const Mystring &rhs) const;

bool operator==(const Mystring &rhs) const;
```

### Global operators

```cpp
friend bool operator==(const Mystring &lhs,
                       const Mystring &rhs);

friend Mystring operator-(const Mystring &obj);

friend Mystring operator+(const Mystring &lhs,
                          const Mystring &rhs);
```

The global version allows expressions such as:

```cpp
"Larry" + moe;
```

which is the main practical motivation demonstrated in this exercise.

